{"actionable":true,"affected_area":"frontend/accessibility","complexity":"small","needs_clarification":false,"priority":"high","questions":[],"summary":"Footer filter buttons in the web app lack proper ARIA attributes for screen reader users to identify the currently selected filter.","type":"bug"}

Background task task_b066ed9eeb91d64e39e80b41 (analyst) is completed.

Result:
{"acceptance_criteria":["1. Each filter button (`all`, `active`, `completed`) includes an `aria-pressed` attribute that is `true` for the active filter and `false` for the others.","2. The visual appearance of the selected button remains unchanged (e.g., same styling as before).","3. A screen reader announces the selected state of each button (e.g., \"all, toggle button, pressed\" for the active filter and \"active, toggle button, not pressed\" for the others).","4. The `active` class is no longer used for styling the selected button (if CSS was updated to use `[aria-pressed=\"true\"]`).","5. The functionality of the buttons (e.g., filtering todos) remains unchanged."],"affected_surface":["/workspace/repo/apps/web/src/components/Footer.tsx (button elements and their attributes)","/workspace/repo/apps/web/src/components/Footer.css or equivalent stylesheet (if the `.active` CSS selector is updated to `[aria-pressed=\"true\"]`)"],"approach":"Use the `aria-pressed` attribute to explicitly communicate the selected state of each filter button to screen readers. This is the standard ARIA attribute for toggle buttons and is widely supported. The `aria-pressed` attribute will be set to `true` for the currently active filter and `false` for the others. This approach is preferred over `aria-current` because these buttons represent a toggle state rather than a navigation or selection in a sequence (e.g., pagination).","artifact_id":null,"assumptions":["1. The repository uses a modern frontend framework (e.g., React) and supports ARIA attributes without issues.","2. The existing CSS for the `.active` class can be safely replaced with `[aria-pressed=\"true\"]` without breaking other parts of the application.","3. The screen reader testing will be performed by the implementer or QA team using standard tools like NVDA, VoiceOver, or JAWS."],"open_questions":["1. Is there a dedicated stylesheet for the `Footer` component (e.g., `Footer.css`), or are the styles defined elsewhere (e.g., a global CSS file)?","2. Are there any existing unit or integration tests for the `Footer` component that can be extended to cover the `aria-pressed` attribute?"],"plan":["1. Add `aria-pressed={option === filter}` to each filter button in `Footer.tsx`. This ensures the attribute reflects the current active filter.","2. Remove the `active` class from the button elements, as it is redundant for accessibility purposes and the visual styling can be preserved via the existing CSS (if needed, update the CSS selector to target `[aria-pressed=\"true\"]` instead of `.active`).","3. Verify that the visual appearance of the buttons remains unchanged after the CSS update (if applicable).","4. Test the buttons with a screen reader to confirm the selected state is announced correctly."],"problem_statement":"The filter buttons in the footer (`all`, `active`, `completed`) visually indicate the selected state using a CSS class (`active`), but this state is not announced to screen readers. This fails WCAG Success Criterion 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), which requires that all user interface components provide appropriate state information programmatically.","risks":["1. **CSS Styling Breakage**: If the visual styling relies on the `.active` class, updating the CSS selector to `[aria-pressed=\"true\"]` is required. Failure to do so may result in the selected button losing its visual distinction. Mitigation: Check the stylesheet for `.active` selectors and update them if necessary.","2. **Regression in Visual Feedback**: The visual feedback for the selected button must remain intact. Mitigation: Manually verify the visual appearance after the change.","3. **Screen Reader Compatibility**: While `aria-pressed` is widely supported, some older screen readers may not handle it perfectly. Mitigation: Test with modern screen readers like NVDA, VoiceOver, or JAWS."],"test_strategy":"1. **Unit Test**: Add or update a unit test in the repository's test suite (e.g., using Jest) to verify that the `aria-pressed` attribute is correctly set for the active filter button and `false` for the others. Example test: render the `Footer` component with each filter (`all`, `active`, `completed`) and assert the `aria-pressed` value of each button.\n2. **Integration Test**: Test the `Footer` component in the context of the full application to ensure the buttons work as expected and the selected state is visually and programmatically correct.\n3. **Manual Accessibility Test**: Use a screen reader (e.g., NVDA, VoiceOver) to verify that the selected state of each button is announced correctly.\n4. **Visual Regression Test**: Manually verify that the visual appearance of the selected button is unchanged."}

